WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-CA

00:00:04.849 --> 00:00:10.689
them receive citations. And the biggest mistake&nbsp;
that holds them back is that they blindly follow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.689 --> 00:00:14.929
the rulebooks of academic writing style.
But the real tragedy is not that they don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.929 --> 00:00:20.329
get citations; it’s that their research doesn’t&nbsp;
impact all the lives that it was destined to.&nbsp;

00:00:20.329 --> 00:00:25.169
Imagine if Watson &amp; Crick’s discovery of the&nbsp;
structure of DNA had never been published;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.169 --> 00:00:30.729
there would be less understanding of genetic&nbsp;
disease, no CRISPR, and no personalized medicine.&nbsp;

00:00:30.729 --> 00:00:35.809
Fortunately, Watson and Crick were good&nbsp;
writers, and their use of academic style&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.809 --> 00:00:40.729
is one of the reasons why their discovery had&nbsp;
such a great impact on the scientific community.&nbsp;

00:00:40.729 --> 00:00:45.649
So today we’re going to follow in their&nbsp;
footsteps by looking at three tips for academic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.649 --> 00:00:50.969
writing style that have been scientifically&nbsp;
proven to increase the likelihood that your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.969 --> 00:00:55.889
research will get published in high impact&nbsp;
journals and cited by others in your field.

00:00:55.889 --> 00:00:59.489
Welcome back to Writer Science, the channel&nbsp;
where we explore the art and science of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.129 --> 00:01:03.449
professional and academic writing.If you’re&nbsp;
new here, my name is William and I’m an English&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.449 --> 00:01:08.569
professor based in South Korea, and for the&nbsp;
writing tips in this video come from an analysis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.569 --> 00:01:15.649
of over 30,000 articles with high impact ratings&nbsp;
– that is, those published in top journals or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.649 --> 00:01:21.049
which received a ton of citations. And the results&nbsp;
might surprise you – they certainly surprised me.

00:01:21.049 --> 00:01:27.449
Now of course, scientific ideas spread because&nbsp;
they are valuable. But let me ask you this:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.449 --> 00:01:41.009
If a tree falls in the forest and&nbsp;
nobody hears it, does it make a sound?&nbsp;

00:01:41.009 --> 00:01:46.089
And if you make a discovery and nobody&nbsp;
reads about it, does it make an impact?&nbsp;

00:01:46.089 --> 00:01:50.449
The main reason why so many academic researchers&nbsp;
don’t get the attention they deserve is not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.449 --> 00:01:54.849
because their work isn’t valuable; it’s&nbsp;
because of the biggest mistake going:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.849 --> 00:02:00.649
they just blindly follow the academic writing&nbsp;
rulebooks and never learn how to use academic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.649 --> 00:02:05.249
writing style effectively, and for that&nbsp;
reason their ideas are dead in the water.

00:02:05.249 --> 00:02:08.769
Luckily, the first common mistake&nbsp;
to avoid is pretty straightforward:

00:02:08.769 --> 00:02:13.129
In the propagation of ideas, it’s&nbsp;
essential to prioritize the cognitive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.129 --> 00:02:18.169
accessibility of the discourse over the&nbsp;
display of the originator's erudition.&nbsp;

00:02:18.169 --> 00:02:20.929
Got it? Good. Moving on. The second mistake is…

00:02:20.929 --> 00:02:25.649
According to the rule books, academic writing&nbsp;
is supposed to be complex. If it’s too simple,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.649 --> 00:02:29.769
they say, then it will make the ideas&nbsp;
seem simple and unimportant, and they’ll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.769 --> 00:02:34.049
never get published in high-impact journals.
And it’s actually true that the complexity of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.049 --> 00:02:39.489
your writing has a big effect on whether the&nbsp;
research will be accepted for publication,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.489 --> 00:02:42.529
except that it’s in exactly&nbsp;
the opposite direction.

00:02:42.529 --> 00:02:47.609
This study by Oppenheimer took a group of&nbsp;
original essays, and then kept the ideas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.609 --> 00:02:52.649
the same but increased the complexity of&nbsp;
the language. If the rule books are right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.649 --> 00:02:57.409
then you would expect that the more complex&nbsp;
articles would have higher acceptance rates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.409 --> 00:03:02.529
than the simpler originals. But exactly&nbsp;
the opposite is true. The essays with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.529 --> 00:03:08.569
highest complexity were rated worse than&nbsp;
the moderately complex ones, which in turn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.569 --> 00:03:16.809
were rated worse than the simple originals.
And this is true across genres. From college&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.809 --> 00:03:21.289
admissions essays to research reports, simpler&nbsp;
language leads to higher acceptance rates.

00:03:21.289 --> 00:03:25.609
And to show you why that is, I need&nbsp;
you to consider the way ideas spread.

00:03:25.609 --> 00:03:31.049
If an idea is carried by complex language,&nbsp;
then it will never make it off the page&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.049 --> 00:03:35.169
and into the mind of the reader. That’s&nbsp;
because there’s only so much work that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.169 --> 00:03:39.489
a reader is willing to do to unpack&nbsp;
your writing and find your message.

00:03:39.489 --> 00:03:44.929
On the other hand, when an idea is presented&nbsp;
in clear language, it can move easily from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.929 --> 00:03:50.209
the page to the mind of our reader. And&nbsp;
when the reader can grasp our idea easily,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.209 --> 00:03:55.129
they’ll be more likely to carry it and pass&nbsp;
it on to others in the form of citation.&nbsp;

00:03:55.129 --> 00:04:01.209
Many academic writers like to dress up their ideas&nbsp;
in complex language in order to make themselves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.209 --> 00:04:06.209
appear smart, but this just turns off the reader.&nbsp;
So the goal of our writing should not be to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.209 --> 00:04:12.129
ourselves seem smart; instead, we should write&nbsp;
in a way that makes the reader feel smart.&nbsp;

00:04:12.129 --> 00:04:15.769
Now one thing I have to make clear here&nbsp;
is that we’re not talking about dumbing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.769 --> 00:04:20.569
down our ideas. We don’t want to talk to&nbsp;
the reader like a fifth grader. What we’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.569 --> 00:04:26.249
talking about is packaging our ideas in a way&nbsp;
that will be easier for the reader to process.&nbsp;

00:04:26.249 --> 00:04:30.729
But how do we reduce complexity?
This is our idea; and this is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.729 --> 00:04:36.329
DNA of the idea. Except we don't&nbsp;
call it DNA; we call it Syntax.

00:04:36.329 --> 00:04:42.969
Syntax is like the DNA of an idea because&nbsp;
it’s code we use to share and replicate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.969 --> 00:04:47.369
ideas. If the code is complex, then&nbsp;
there are going to be mutations in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.369 --> 00:04:52.609
the DNA and the idea won’t make&nbsp;
it into the mind of the reader.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.609 --> 00:04:58.009
So we need to make sure the code is as&nbsp;
simple and straightforward as possible.

00:04:58.009 --> 00:05:01.489
Here's the same idea presented&nbsp;
in two different codes.

00:05:01.489 --> 00:05:03.049
Which one is easier to understand?

00:05:03.049 --> 00:05:09.169
It's the second one, of course, because in&nbsp;
the DNA of ideas, there are only two elements:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.689 --> 00:05:12.529
content words (like nouns, verbs, and adjectives);&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.129 --> 00:05:16.689
and function words (like&nbsp;
prepositions and determiners).&nbsp;

00:05:16.689 --> 00:05:23.849
Each of these codes use the same content words;&nbsp;
but one uses way more function words than the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.849 --> 00:05:30.409
other. When a sentence has a lot of these function&nbsp;
words, it becomes heavy with all these branches.

00:05:30.409 --> 00:05:33.449
Academic articles written with fewer articles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.449 --> 00:05:38.129
and fewer prepositions in the&nbsp;
introduction get more citations.&nbsp;

00:05:38.129 --> 00:05:42.689
The fewer function words, the simpler the&nbsp;
sentence; and the simpler the sentence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.689 --> 00:05:48.849
the easier it is for the reader to get our idea&nbsp;
and the more likely they will be to share that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.849 --> 00:05:53.169
idea with others, which brings us to the&nbsp;
second rule about academic writing style.

00:05:53.169 --> 00:05:58.249
According to the rule books, academic writing&nbsp;
should be formal and impersonal. After all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.249 --> 00:06:00.529
we’re not talking about literature here;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.529 --> 00:06:05.729
this is science, and science is objective.&nbsp;
This objectivity, according to the rule book,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.729 --> 00:06:10.769
should be reflected in the writing style&nbsp;
itself. It doesn’t matter who does the research;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.769 --> 00:06:14.609
what matters is the research itself.
That’s why so many academic writers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.609 --> 00:06:20.169
choose to use passive sentences and avoid&nbsp;
the use of personal pronouns. Pssst... and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.169 --> 00:06:23.529
it’s also why their research fails to&nbsp;
pick up publications and citations.

00:06:23.529 --> 00:06:29.329
To understand why impersonal language doesn’t&nbsp;
work in academic writing, take a look at this:&nbsp;

00:06:29.329 --> 00:06:33.689
It's a Klein bottle, and it represents a&nbsp;
mathematical surface that has no edges or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.689 --> 00:06:37.689
orientation - no outside, and no&nbsp;
inside. It can be thought of as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.689 --> 00:06:42.489
higher-dimension analogue to the Mobius&nbsp;
strip, but, unlike the Mobius strip,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.489 --> 00:06:47.769
which has only one side and one boundary,&nbsp;
the Klein bottle has no boundaries at all.&nbsp;

00:06:47.769 --> 00:06:53.649
Now compare that description of the Klein&nbsp;
bottle with this one, from a popular TED talk.&nbsp;

00:07:25.049 --> 00:07:27.597
Which presentation are you&nbsp;
more likely to remember?&nbsp;

00:07:27.597 --> 00:07:27.683
The information is the same, but when it’s&nbsp;
presented by a researcher who is clearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.683 --> 00:07:27.769
passionate about the Klein bottle, it’s a&nbsp;
lot easier for us to share that interest.

00:07:27.769 --> 00:07:31.569
People relate to other people, and&nbsp;
readers are people, so when there’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.569 --> 00:07:36.529
a person present in the story, it makes&nbsp;
it a lot easier for the reader to connect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.529 --> 00:07:40.449
to the story and receive the ideas.&nbsp;
And that connection makes it a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.449 --> 00:07:44.929
easier for the writer to persuade the&nbsp;
reader that the research is valuable.

00:07:44.929 --> 00:07:50.449
Now it’s important to note that this doesn't work&nbsp;
in every place throughout the entire article.&nbsp;

00:07:50.449 --> 00:07:53.249
Personal language only helps&nbsp;
with citations when they’re in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.249 --> 00:07:54.289
the front matter, which is the&nbsp;
introduction and the abstract.

00:07:54.289 --> 00:07:59.689
By contrast, articles that use personal&nbsp;
language in the middle section–the methods&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.689 --> 00:08:05.209
and results–actually receive fewer citations.
And to understand why that is, we need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.209 --> 00:08:09.769
consider the purpose of these sections.
The purpose of the mid section is to unpack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.769 --> 00:08:14.609
the finer details of the experiment itself.&nbsp;
It’s sometimes known as the empirical section.

00:08:14.609 --> 00:08:18.529
At this point of the report, the reader&nbsp;
doesn’t care about the story; instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.529 --> 00:08:21.289
readers come to this section to examine&nbsp;
the author’s methods and findings.&nbsp;

00:08:21.289 --> 00:08:27.409
So it makes sense that the most successful authors&nbsp;
would use passive and impersonal sentences,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.649 --> 00:08:33.589
which hides the presence of the&nbsp;
author and highlight the work itself.&nbsp;

00:08:33.589 --> 00:08:37.569
On the other hand, the purpose of&nbsp;
the front matter–the abstract and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.569 --> 00:08:42.089
introduction–is to motivate our reader,&nbsp;
to get them interested in our research.

00:08:42.089 --> 00:08:46.529
If we introduce this research using&nbsp;
impersonal language, then it’s like the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.529 --> 00:08:51.249
Klein bottle; the reader’s not going to care.
But like the mad scientist in the TED Talk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.249 --> 00:08:56.489
the most successful articles use personal&nbsp;
language in the abstract and introduction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.489 --> 00:09:01.889
in order to capture the reader’s interest and&nbsp;
motivate them to read the rest of the report.&nbsp;

00:09:01.889 --> 00:09:05.729
But not only can personal language&nbsp;
help capture the reader’s interest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.729 --> 00:09:09.724
it also changes the way the reader perceives you.
When you take personal ownership of ideas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.724 --> 00:09:12.329
it makes you seem more authoritative. If you&nbsp;
introduce valuable research with personal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.329 --> 00:09:18.609
language, readers will associate that value with&nbsp;
you, making you seem more credible and important.&nbsp;

00:09:18.609 --> 00:09:23.049
So if your work is any good, then don’t be&nbsp;
shy; let the reader know that it’s yours,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.049 --> 00:09:26.209
especially in the front matter.
If you follow the lousy rule books&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.209 --> 00:09:31.729
and stick with impersonal language, your&nbsp;
research will appear lifeless and detached,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.729 --> 00:09:36.529
as though the ideas–your ideas–are&nbsp;
just out there floating in the ether.

00:09:36.529 --> 00:09:40.609
But to understand the third and final tip&nbsp;
about academic writing style, I want you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.609 --> 00:09:46.289
to take a look at these two restaurant&nbsp;
reviews. Which one seems more appetizing?

00:09:53.809 --> 00:09:58.809
Many people would agree that the one written&nbsp;
in the present tense sounds more convincing.

00:09:58.809 --> 00:10:00.769
But if the present tense is more convincing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.769 --> 00:10:06.009
then why do so many academic rule books tell us&nbsp;
to write about research using the past tense?

00:10:06.009 --> 00:10:10.689
A lot of people think that they have to use&nbsp;
the past tense to talk about research in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.689 --> 00:10:15.489
past. Makes sense, right? It's the past tense;&nbsp;
research happened in the past. But that's wrong.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.489 --> 00:10:18.969
It's kind of a deeper grammatical point that&nbsp;
we don't have to get into here, but the basic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.969 --> 00:10:23.649
message is that tense has nothing to do with&nbsp;
time, and there's no rule that says you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.649 --> 00:10:28.689
to use the past tense to talk about research&nbsp;
just because it happened in the past. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.689 --> 00:10:32.889
there are some really good reasons to use&nbsp;
the present tense whenever you talk about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.889 --> 00:10:38.169
your own research. When we divorce tense&nbsp;
from time, it becomes really easy to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.169 --> 00:10:43.889
why authors who write about their research&nbsp;
in the present tense receive more citations.&nbsp;

00:10:43.889 --> 00:10:50.529
Just like the restaurant reviews, the present&nbsp;
tense makes research seem more current, relevant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.529 --> 00:10:56.689
and applicable. The past tense, sounds like&nbsp;
old news; it's passé, stale, and forgotten.

00:10:56.689 --> 00:11:00.889
So when writing about your own research,&nbsp;
using the present tense will make it seem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.889 --> 00:11:06.329
more current, more alive, which makes it&nbsp;
more likely to get picked up for citation.&nbsp;

00:11:06.329 --> 00:11:12.609
Yes, the research happened in the past; but if we&nbsp;
follow the rulebooks and write about our research&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.609 --> 00:11:17.409
using the so-called past tense, then&nbsp;
it’s going to seem like old news,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.409 --> 00:11:21.489
so breath life into your research&nbsp;
by using the present tense.

00:11:21.489 --> 00:11:25.849
So I really hope you see that, if your research&nbsp;
isn't getting the attention it deserves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.849 --> 00:11:30.209
it’s really not your fault; it’s the&nbsp;
fault of the old academic rule books.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.209 --> 00:11:34.089
But to tell you the truth, if it's&nbsp;
citations that you’re looking for,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.089 --> 00:11:39.249
you would actually be better off not writing&nbsp;
a research report at all and instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.249 --> 00:11:43.369
you need to check out this video right here&nbsp;
because it’s going to show you how to write&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.369 --> 00:11:50.209
a different kind of academic article that has&nbsp;
been shown to get cited way more often and over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.209 --> 00:12:00.329
longer periods of time than the standard IMRaD&nbsp;
research report, so be sure to go check it out.

